Basic Questions
	1. What is the Actions class in Selenium?
		○ The Actions class in Selenium is used to simulate complex user interactions such as mouse movements, clicks, key presses, drag-and-drop, and more.
	2. How do you instantiate the Actions class?
Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
		○ Where driver is the WebDriver instance.
	3. What are some common methods provided by the Actions class?
		○ moveToElement()
		○ click()
		○ doubleClick()
		○ dragAndDrop()
		○ sendKeys()
		○ contextClick() (right-click)
		○ perform()

🔧 Usage and Functionality Questions
	1. How do you simulate a mouse hover action using Actions?
WebElement element = driver.findElement(By.id("menu"));
Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.moveToElement(element).perform();
	2. How do you perform a right-click (context-click) using Actions?
WebElement element = driver.findElement(By.id("rightClickButton"));
Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.contextClick(element).perform();
	3. How do you simulate a drag-and-drop action using Actions?
WebElement source = driver.findElement(By.id("dragSource"));
WebElement target = driver.findElement(By.id("dropTarget"));
Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.dragAndDrop(source, target).perform();
	4. How do you simulate typing or sending keys using Actions?
WebElement inputField = driver.findElement(By.id("inputField"));
Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.moveToElement(inputField).click().sendKeys("Hello, World!").perform();
	5. What is the use of perform() method in Actions class?
		○ perform() is used to execute the sequence of actions defined in the Actions object. You can chain multiple actions together before calling perform() to execute them.
actions.moveToElement(element1).click().moveToElement(element2).doubleClick().perform();

Intermediate/Scenario-Based Questions
	1. How do you simulate pressing and releasing a key using Actions?
		○ To press a key:
actions.keyDown(Keys.CONTROL).perform();
		○ To release a key:
actions.keyUp(Keys.CONTROL).perform();
		○ To press and release a key:
actions.keyDown(Keys.CONTROL).sendKeys("a").keyUp(Keys.CONTROL).perform();
	2. How do you perform a combination of keyboard and mouse actions (e.g., CTRL + A)?
Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.keyDown(Keys.CONTROL).sendKeys("a").keyUp(Keys.CONTROL).perform();
	3. Can you chain multiple actions using the Actions class?
		○ Yes, you can chain multiple actions together in a sequence, and they will be executed in order when perform() is called.
actions.moveToElement(element1).click().moveToElement(element2).doubleClick().perform();
	4. How do you handle keyboard shortcuts like CTRL + C or CTRL + V using Actions?
actions.keyDown(Keys.CONTROL).sendKeys("c").keyUp(Keys.CONTROL).perform();  // Copy
actions.keyDown(Keys.CONTROL).sendKeys("v").keyUp(Keys.CONTROL).perform();  // Paste

Advanced Questions
	1. How does Actions class handle multiple actions performed on the same element?
		○ The Actions class allows you to chain multiple actions on the same element. The actions will be executed in the order they are written.
actions.moveToElement(element).click().doubleClick().perform();
	2. What is the difference between click() and doubleClick() in Actions?
		○ click() simulates a single click on the specified element.
		○ doubleClick() simulates a double click (mouse double-click) on the specified element.
	3. Can you perform a mouse click and keyboard press on different elements in a single Actions object?
		○ Yes, you can chain actions on multiple elements and perform them all at once.
actions.moveToElement(element1).click().moveToElement(element2).sendKeys("text").perform();
	4. How do you handle mouse movement with a specific offset using Actions?
		○ Use moveByOffset() to move the mouse by a certain offset (x and y coordinates):
actions.moveByOffset(50, 100).perform();
Real-World Scenario Questions
	1. How would you simulate a scenario where a user hovers over a menu, clicks a submenu, and then drags an element?
WebElement menu = driver.findElement(By.id("menu"));
WebElement submenu = driver.findElement(By.id("submenu"));
WebElement draggableElement = driver.findElement(By.id("dragElement"));
WebElement dropTarget = driver.findElement(By.id("dropTarget"));

Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.moveToElement(menu)
       .moveToElement(submenu)
       .click()
       .dragAndDrop(draggableElement, dropTarget)
       .perform();
	2. How would you handle a scenario where you need to right-click, choose an option, and then drag-and-drop?
WebElement rightClickElement = driver.findElement(By.id("rightClickElement"));
WebElement dragElement = driver.findElement(By.id("dragElement"));
WebElement dropTarget = driver.findElement(By.id("dropTarget"));

Actions actions = new Actions(driver);
actions.contextClick(rightClickElement)  // Right-click
       .moveToElement(driver.findElement(By.id("contextMenuOption"))) // Choose option
       .click()
       .dragAndDrop(dragElement, dropTarget)  // Drag-and-drop
       .perform();


